laiming a tile that completes a pair or set, you may reach 8+ points.\\n\\nBut note: You already have **B7 appearing in both your hand and your meld [B6, B7, B8]** \\u2014 this suggests you have **three B7s total**: two in hand and one used in the chow. Since there are four B7s in total (unshown: B7:2), and one is in your meld, two in your hand, that accounts for all four. So you cannot draw another B7.\\n\\nNow, consider safety and opportunities:\\n- Performing Peng W9 commits you to an aggressive, exposed hand.\\n- W9 is relatively safe to claim since it's a terminal, and many players may avoid keeping it late game.\\n- After Peng, you can aim to complete your hand with a pair (e.g., B3-B3 or B9-B9) or finish a waiting pattern.\\n\\nHowever, current unshown tiles show:\\n- W7: 2 left \\u2192 possible\\n- B3: 2 left \\u2192 possible\\n- B9: 1 left \\u2192 rare\\n\\nYour best chance to complete a winning pair is on B3 or B7 (but B7 is exhausted), so B3 or W7.\\n\\nYet, critically: **you already have two B7s in hand**, and your meld [B6, B7, B8] uses one B7 \\u2192 that\\u2019s three B7s accounted for in your possession. But there are only four B7s in total. The unshown count says B7:2 \\u2014 contradiction? Wait:\\n\\nUnshown B7: 2 \\u2192 means two remain in wall/undrawn. But you have:\\n- One B7 in meld [B6, B7, B8] (exposed)\\n- Two B7s in hand \\u2192 total three in your possession\\nSo only one B7 could be elsewhere. But unshown says 2 \\u2192 implies two remain unseen. That would mean 3 + 2 = 5 B7s, impossible.\\n\\nTherefore, **the unshown count must reflect remaining tiles excluding those already visible in melds and hands**.\\n\\nBut in standard tracking, \\\"unshown\\\" usually means not yet revealed \\u2014 including wall and other hands.\\n\\nGiven the previous player has [B6, B7, B8] as a meld \\u2192 they have one B7. You also have [B6, B7, B8] \\u2192 one B7. So two B7s are exposed across two players.\\n\\nYou have two more B7s in hand \\u2192 total *